Q: How can I make my carousel transition smooth? A: <p>as fas as i understood your code, you are removing the old cards and creating new ones again (basically removing elements from dom and creating new elements to achieve slider like animation). that causes style recalculation, layout recalculation and repainting on every slide change, which is heavy for the browser.</p>
<p>Instead, try using transform translateX to move the slides as CSS works better when elements are already their. Even better, you can use a library like Swiperjs for smoother and more optimized animations.</p>
<p><strong>but here's the fix if you want to DIY:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>the core problem is this line inside update():</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>prevSlot.innerHTML = wrapPeek(slides[prevIndex].html);
currSlot.innerHTML = wrapSlot(slides[index].html);
nextSlot.innerHTML = wrapPeek(slides[nextIndex].html);
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>to fix it:</strong></li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>stop replacing innerHTML on every navigation</li>
<li>instead of injecting HTML in update(), you should:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Create all slides once</li>
<li>keep them in the DOM</li>
<li>Only change the transform</li>
</ul>
<ol start="3">
<li><strong>Remove this whole part:</strong></li>
</ol>
<pre><code>function update() {

const prevIndex = mod(index - 1, slides.length);
const nextIndex = mod(index + 1, slides.length);

prevSlot.innerHTML = wrapPeek(slides[prevIndex].html);
currSlot.innerHTML = wrapSlot(slides[index].html);
nextSlot.innerHTML = wrapPeek(slides[nextIndex].html);
renderDots();
}
</code></pre>
<p>instead, build slots once</p>
<p>Add this before <code>update()</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>slides.forEach(s =&gt; {
  const el = document.createElement(&quot;div&quot;);
  el.className = &quot;slot-inner&quot;;
  el.innerHTML = s.html;
  currSlot.appendChild(el);
});
</code></pre>
<p>Then remove <code>wrapSlot</code> and <code>wrapPeek</code> usage entirely.</p>
<ol start="4">
<li><strong>Replace <code>update()</code> with transform logic:</strong></li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function update() {

  const width = currSlot.offsetWidth;

  currSlot.style.transform = `translateX(-${index * width}px)`;

  renderDots();
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="5">
<li><strong>Remove absolute positioning.</strong></li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.peek .slot-inner,
.center .slot-inner {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="6">
<li><strong>add this instead:</strong></li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.center {
  overflow: hidden;
}

.center {
  display: flex;
  transition: transform 0.9s cubic-bezier(.22,.61,.36,1);
  will-change: transform;
}

.slot-inner {
  flex: 0 0 100%;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>free advise: just use the swiperjs like libraries instead</strong></p>

Q: Mongoose &amp; TypeScript A: <p>The problem occurs because <code>SchemaOptions</code> composes virtuals and timestamps at the type level, and Mongoose's inference engine can't really reconcile how <code>DefaultTimestampProps</code> interacts with the virtual populate's type signature. When both are in <code>SchemaOptions</code>, the conflict propagates to the inferred <code>this</code> type for static methods.</p>
<p>You could <strong>Use <code>schema.virtual()</code> + <code>schema.static()</code> to fix it:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>
interface IssueOneDoc {
  placeholder?: string;
}

const issueOneSchema = new mongoose.Schema&lt;IssueOneDoc&gt;(
  { placeholder: String },
  { timestamps: true }
);

// Define virtual after schema creation
issueOneSchema.virtual('votes', {
  ref: 'IssueTwo',
  localField: '_id',
  foreignField: 'issueOneId'
});

// Define static after schema creation
issueOneSchema.static('myStaticMethod', function () {
  console.log('placeholder');
});

const IssueOne = mongoose.model('IssueOne', issueOneSchema);

IssueOne.myStaticMethod(); 
</code></pre>
